1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,760
Today's operation: “Hey, we're doing things our way!”

2
00:00:05,760 --> 00:00:18,160
This operation features the ever-busy voice actress Kaede Hondo, whose voice you hear every day, and Hantaro Tenshin, who keeps paying for meals because of promises he doesn't remember making.

3
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:23,820
It's a lavish banquet of a show brought to you by this miraculous duo who can't even find time to eat together.

4
00:00:23,820 --> 00:00:28,540
Kaede Hondo and Hantaro Tenshin's Hondo Landing Operation.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,000
[Music]

6
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:44,380
Hi, I'm Kaede Hondo. I'm Hantaro Amatsu. Good evening. Wasn't that last bit really long? It was. Honestly, I'd let my guard down.

7
00:00:44,380 --> 00:00:52,180
I thought I could get through it easily, and then had to rush through the end. Sorry.

8
00:00:52,180 --> 00:00:58,320
That show introduction came from Kagurazaka Tama. Thank you.

9
00:00:58,320 --> 00:01:07,400
“A busy voice actress whose voice you hear every day.” That's exactly right. Not really, but thank you. You're doing so well. I'll keep trying.

10
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:21,460
As for me, “paying because of promises I don't remember making”—that whole joke about making me pay has started to settle down.

11
00:01:21,460 --> 00:01:31,440
I wonder why. It's gone from a playful “Why do I have to pay?!” to a serious “Why do I have to pay...?”

12
00:01:31,440 --> 00:01:38,740
Did people pick up on that and stop asking you to treat them? Maybe that's it.

13
00:01:38,740 --> 00:01:50,300
We keep saying, “Let's go out for a meal with the show's team,” but we never manage to eat together.

14
00:01:50,300 --> 00:01:57,640
Honestly, we're all adults. The staff, the writer, the director—everyone.

15
00:01:57,640 --> 00:02:05,980
So I'm starting to think we really could just split the bill. Me too?

16
00:02:06,940 --> 00:02:13,240
You too, Hondo. Yes! I'm being recognized as an adult!

17
00:02:13,240 --> 00:02:18,780
For a long time you wouldn't even let me pay my share. Though I appreciated it.

18
00:02:18,780 --> 00:02:25,980
Director, writer, what do you think? Shall we hear your thoughts? Splitting the bill sounds fine, doesn't it?

19
00:02:25,980 --> 00:02:35,700
If you don't want to split it, say so now. No reply means you're okay with it, right? Then it's settled.

20
00:02:35,700 --> 00:02:43,460
Yes! Where did they go? Sorry, here's the thing.

21
00:02:43,460 --> 00:02:51,540
Neither the director nor the writer is here again today. It's really unusual for both to be absent.

22
00:02:51,540 --> 00:02:59,300
The director has someone filling in. Thanks as always. But the writer doesn't even have a substitute.

23
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:09,520
There's just a plastic bottle sitting there. That's to keep cats away. Why is there a bottle over there?

24
00:03:09,520 --> 00:03:20,540
It's half empty. There's a bottle sitting there, but today it's just the two of us and the director who usually fills in.

25
00:03:20,540 --> 00:03:31,780
So today's goal is, “Hey, we're doing things our way.” We'll do whatever we want for the rest of the show.

26
00:03:31,780 --> 00:03:41,520
Normally the writer and director carefully plan the content, so it's not for us performers to complain. But they're not here today.

27
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:53,740
Since we have the chance, we'd like to do the things we want. Please stay with us until the end.

28
00:03:53,740 --> 00:04:00,400
Kaede Hondo and Hantaro Tenshin's Hondo Landing Operation. I just said “Hantaro.” Here we go!

29
00:04:00,400 --> 00:04:09,480
This program is brought to you by Anime Blast.

30
00:04:10,200 --> 00:04:18,060
Is this Anime Blast? The place we landed was the legendary land of Anime Blast.

31
00:04:18,060 --> 00:04:25,160
Here, we'll discover new ways to enjoy anime. Our dreams are far from over.

32
00:04:25,160 --> 00:04:33,180
Let's go, Hantaro! Search for “Anime Blast” to find out more! Are you ready?

33
00:04:34,760 --> 00:04:38,560
I'm Kaede Hondo. I'm Hantaro Tenshin. Hondo Landing Operation.

34
00:04:38,560 --> 00:04:46,420
I'm Kaede Hondo. I'm Hantaro Tenshin. Peace, peace!

35
00:04:46,420 --> 00:04:57,540
Kagurazaka Tama, who sent the introduction, adds: “P.S. This is my first message. I always enjoy listening. I hope you can all eat together soon.”

36
00:04:57,540 --> 00:05:04,660
“You sound like you're such good friends; listening warms my heart.” Thank you. Warm and fuzzy.

37
00:05:04,660 --> 00:05:15,260
Do people see us as good friends? Is that the impression this show gives? Which is it?

38
00:05:15,260 --> 00:05:21,520
I guess, despite everything, we do look close. Fair enough.

39
00:05:21,520 --> 00:05:28,380
We've known the staff for over ten years and still want to go out for a meal, so yes, we must get along.

40
00:05:28,380 --> 00:05:38,080
There was only one person left, right? Only the writer hadn't responded. The director finally got things moving.

41
00:05:38,080 --> 00:05:44,000
Was it “Schedule-kun”? Choseisan, maybe. Choseisan! That's completely different.

42
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:54,820
They entered everyone's availability in Choseisan, and so far there's one day that works. We just need to know whether the writer can make it.

43
00:05:54,820 --> 00:06:05,980
Still no answer? Can't get a signal? Maybe they were planning to tell us in person today. We don't know, since they're not here.

44
00:06:05,980 --> 00:06:16,500
Maybe the day will come when we all get together and split the bill for dinner. We settled the splitting-the-bill part today.

45
00:06:16,500 --> 00:06:24,640
We also decided that the director pays the remainder. The director, not the writer? Well, I don't know.

46
00:06:24,640 --> 00:06:37,820
The most grown-up person can pay. Who's the oldest on this team? Isn't it the director? Then the director will do.

47
00:06:37,820 --> 00:06:42,020
We'd like to tell you about it here too. Stay tuned.

48
00:06:42,020 --> 00:06:48,700
As we said, we're doing things our way. We'll do what we want!

49
00:06:48,700 --> 00:06:58,900
We mentioned at the event that we don't read enough listener messages. We promised we'd read them.

50
00:06:58,900 --> 00:07:10,580
We did one episode reading lots of messages, almost as a token effort. But once the immediate concern passed, we stopped again. That's not good.

51
00:07:10,580 --> 00:07:16,060
This time, we're reading messages. Let's read them! This is...

52
00:07:16,060 --> 00:07:24,800
If the director and writer were here, that might not happen. The poetry segment's background music would already be playing now. They'd start it.

53
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:37,620
They could play that music, but we'd definitely get comments that I talk too much. I think I've seen that on YouTube. It always comes up in the comments.

54
00:07:37,620 --> 00:07:48,120
Should I talk more? But this is how we both naturally are. It's a tricky balance. It really is.

55
00:07:48,120 --> 00:08:01,820
You'll have to take the show as it is. But we do read the comments. There's your warning! Let me be clear: I read them. I check them now and then too.

56
00:08:01,820 --> 00:08:13,760
It's good if knowing we read them makes you reconsider a comment. But deliberately writing insults because you know we'll see them is the worst. Please don't do that.

57
00:08:13,760 --> 00:08:23,000
Please be kind. Warm-hearted! We're about to read messages with kind hearts. We absolutely will.

58
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:30,720
This is from Kidokuratchi Tantousha; thank you. “Kaede and Hantaro, jori!” Jori!

59
00:08:30,720 --> 00:08:37,500
“I'm sorry I've been writing Hantaro's name with the wrong kanji for several years.” That's a long time.

60
00:08:37,500 --> 00:08:45,800
It's only been two years since I changed to Hantaro, so maybe you were getting my old name wrong too. “Please accept my apology.”

61
00:08:45,800 --> 00:08:54,440
“Hantaro has bought unnecessary things online while drunk before. Have either of you made any bad purchases recently?”

62
00:08:54,440 --> 00:09:03,180
They wrote “sorry” in the name and address fields. That takes me back to those profile books we filled out in elementary school.

63
00:09:03,320 --> 00:09:14,600
When something was private, we'd write “sorry,” meaning, “It's a secret.” Maybe that's what this means, though it also ties in with the apology.

64
00:09:14,600 --> 00:09:22,580
I used to buy things while drunk a lot. Isn't the postal code “sorry” too? You're right!

65
00:09:22,580 --> 00:09:29,840
They've used numbers to spell out an apology.

66
00:09:29,840 --> 00:09:36,600
You're not really sorry! Don't do that—we're reading kindly, remember? Sorry. Forgiven? You're forgiven.

67
00:09:36,600 --> 00:09:42,820
What did you buy while drunk again?

68
00:09:42,820 --> 00:09:52,520
I'd wake up, the doorbell would ring, and a whiteboard would arrive. I remember you telling us that.

69
00:09:52,520 --> 00:09:59,800
A proper whiteboard on wheels, like the ones in conference rooms. That was a nuisance.

70
00:09:59,800 --> 00:10:05,980
It sounds useful. Was it in the way at home? It was.

71
00:10:05,980 --> 00:10:17,820
Bad purchases lately... I browse Amazon while drinking less often now. You've cut back? I have.

72
00:10:17,820 --> 00:10:27,660
I don't consider this a mistake—I still think it was a good purchase—but I haven't been riding the bike I bought. I see.

73
00:10:27,660 --> 00:10:32,300
I bought it about a year ago. You've ridden it a few times? I have.

74
00:10:32,300 --> 00:10:43,620
But it's cold, or hot... The heat is tricky, but couldn't pedaling warm you up in the cold? Or it's raining.

75
00:10:43,620 --> 00:10:56,820
Or there's an uphill stretch. Just put some effort in! Or it's tiring. Or I'm making excuses. In the end, I haven't been riding it.

76
00:10:56,820 --> 00:11:08,080
Wasn't it a pretty nice bike? It is. Bike prices can go endlessly higher, but this one cost quite a bit.

77
00:11:08,080 --> 00:11:13,340
Not riding it is a waste. It really is a waste.

78
00:11:13,340 --> 00:11:24,720
I really want to go cycling this year, on a day off, along a riverbank or something. I did when I first bought it.

79
00:11:24,720 --> 00:11:34,680
I need a whole free day to do that. You want a full day. I do.

80
00:11:34,680 --> 00:11:48,420
What about commuting? With a nice bike, the issue is where to leave it. It's scary without proper bike parking? Right.

81
00:11:48,420 --> 00:11:54,980
I hear they'll steal bikes without hesitation even when they're properly locked.

82
00:11:54,980 --> 00:12:08,780
When I bought it, they said, “It's fine to ride somewhere and park it, but don't leave it for a long time.” That's scary.

83
00:12:08,780 --> 00:12:23,640
I asked, “By a long time, do you mean four or five hours?” They said, “No, one hour.” That's not long! Apparently one hour is enough for it to be at risk, so be careful.

84
00:12:23,640 --> 00:12:31,700
How much did that bike cost? Sounds like quite a few digits.

85
00:12:31,700 --> 00:12:42,600
One, ten, a hundred, a thousand... a hundred thousand. A hundred thousand.

86
00:12:42,600 --> 00:12:56,520
A hundred thousand yen. That's a really nice bike by my standards. It is. I hope you get to ride it. Not a bad purchase, though.

87
00:12:56,520 --> 00:13:04,360
I've practically made it a goal for next year. Isn't that early? It's only mid-September!

88
00:13:04,360 --> 00:13:17,460
Bookstores already have planners for sale. Ones starting in September? Maybe October too. So this is the time to buy them.

89
00:13:17,460 --> 00:13:31,720
We need to start thinking about next year. Hondo, any bad purchases? Yes, clothing-related ones.

90
00:13:31,720 --> 00:13:45,760
I'd wanted a T-shirt with a particular design and asked someone to let me know if they found one. They told me they'd found one, possibly in a smaller size.

91
00:13:45,760 --> 00:13:54,400
When I went to buy it, the width, sleeves, and neckline were good, but it was too long. I see.

92
00:13:54,400 --> 00:14:06,680
Different T-shirt brands apparently have different fits. They said it might shrink a little if I washed and dried it. Might being the key word.

93
00:14:06,680 --> 00:14:13,820
Maybe I could tuck it in. But it's still long. I really liked the design, though, so I decided to give it a try and bought it.

94
00:14:13,820 --> 00:14:27,240
I washed it and put it in the dryer, and it shrank a tiny bit, just as they'd said. Barely enough to tell.

95
00:14:27,240 --> 00:14:36,720
It must be good fabric. I wore it, but it was so long there was too much fabric even to tuck in.

96
00:14:36,720 --> 00:14:46,660
I took it as a learning experience and put it back into circulation. I see. That's tricky.

97
00:14:46,660 --> 00:14:56,620
I know nothing about clothes. People who care about fashion pay attention to the length and things like that. We do.

98
00:14:56,620 --> 00:15:05,400
I basically buy the clothes my stylist picks out for me. It's so much easier.

99
00:15:05,400 --> 00:15:12,540
Sometimes I look at what they've brought and think, “Really? This?” You do?

100
00:15:12,540 --> 00:15:19,020
But it isn't my place to butt in about something a professional has chosen. I see.

101
00:15:19,020 --> 00:15:31,460
Even if it doesn't feel like me, I wear it to the recording. Then the makeup artist and all the staff say, “That outfit looks great!”

102
00:15:31,460 --> 00:15:39,480
It's precisely the outfits I'm unsure about that get lots of compliments. Do you have any photos? I don't think so.

103
00:15:39,480 --> 00:15:51,580
So I think I really have no sense of style. The things I like don't get compliments; only the things I'm unsure about do.

104
00:15:51,580 --> 00:15:58,020
That stylist is good. They're amazing. They find aspects of my image that I wouldn't think of myself.

105
00:15:58,020 --> 00:16:05,540
Do you always work with that stylist? Yes. That's great.

106
00:16:05,540 --> 00:16:15,200
It is, but I keep losing confidence in my own taste. You're wearing a matching set today, Hantaro, and it looks really nice.

107
00:16:15,200 --> 00:16:25,100
I'm determined to host anime events all summer and into autumn in this one outfit. Just this one?

108
00:16:26,720 --> 00:16:38,780
I can change the T-shirt underneath; it's a matching set. When I was younger—you'll probably understand if you're into anime...

109
00:16:38,780 --> 00:16:48,780
Black, with skulls. How nostalgic! I'd wear clothes with so many zippers you'd ask, “How many does it need?”

110
00:16:48,780 --> 00:16:58,620
Were the zippers decorative? Yes, but even so, there were too many. I couldn't believe how many there were.

111
00:16:58,620 --> 00:17:07,700
Thinking eyepatches and fingerless gloves were cool. That phase when you admire that look.

112
00:17:07,700 --> 00:17:16,160
I took that phase too far, so it lasted until about 24. Isn't that a long time? It lasted until 24.

113
00:17:16,160 --> 00:17:25,400
I was lucky. My first girlfriend was very stylish and had wanted to become a stylist herself.

114
00:17:25,400 --> 00:17:32,960
That first girlfriend stuffed all my clothes—all those zippers—into garbage bags.

115
00:17:32,960 --> 00:17:39,220
“Shut up,” she said. I hadn't said anything! She took the clothes out and said, “Shut up.” But I wasn't talking!

116
00:17:39,220 --> 00:17:46,720
She picked out cute, cheerful clothes and said, “I think these would suit you.”

117
00:17:46,720 --> 00:17:58,880
At the theater then, the audience voted for the comedy acts they liked. It was supposed to be about how funny you were, but without popularity it was hard to get votes.

118
00:17:58,880 --> 00:18:10,600
If I hadn't met her, people might have thought, “His act's funny, but wow, those zippers. What an outfit.”

119
00:18:10,600 --> 00:18:18,900
And decided not to vote for me. You had that many zippers? You must be grateful to her. Absolutely.

120
00:18:18,900 --> 00:18:26,020
That's the phase I went through. And you look stylish today too, Hondo. I'm glad you think so.

121
00:18:26,020 --> 00:18:39,600
I like the brand Maison Special. I'm wearing one of their denim vests over a black tank top.

122
00:18:39,600 --> 00:18:49,360
The bottoms are secondhand. You can pull that look off so effortlessly. Anyone would look like this if they put it on.

123
00:18:49,360 --> 00:18:58,580
Remember this outfit. Now picture me wearing it. Wouldn't I look like I was doing a Sugi-chan impression at a year-end party?

124
00:18:58,580 --> 00:19:08,400
That's the danger. Now that you say that, I'd like to take back what I said. The outfit would still look like this, though.

125
00:19:08,400 --> 00:19:17,780
But our body shapes are too different. That's true. Get on the bike! I need to lose weight. Thank you.

126
00:19:17,780 --> 00:19:25,200
Let's read another message, from Kusunda Keito. “Hondo and Hantaro, joribanwa!”

127
00:19:25,200 --> 00:19:33,440
“I'm glad your frozen shoulder is recovering well. I started taking exercise classes as an adult...”

128
00:19:33,440 --> 00:19:40,380
“...and really enjoy keeping them up, so I felt a connection with you enjoying jiu-jitsu.”

129
00:19:40,380 --> 00:19:50,440
“I hope you continue to improve. When you return, please tell us some funny jiu-jitsu stories. I'll look forward to them.”

130
00:19:50,440 --> 00:20:03,800
I haven't gone in quite a while. You're taking a kind of leave of absence, right? Yes, I told them I needed a break.

131
00:20:03,800 --> 00:20:13,860
It's their policy, but they removed me from the LINE group everyone belongs to, just because I was taking a break. That caught me off guard.

132
00:20:13,860 --> 00:20:21,460
My shoulder moves much better now, and I'm still going to rehab. How high can you raise your arm? You'll be surprised.

133
00:20:21,460 --> 00:20:30,440
Wow, you can raise it like someone saying, “Hands up!” Yes, I can lift it properly now.

134
00:20:30,440 --> 00:20:41,300
I went to rehab today, and the therapist was surprised.

135
00:20:41,300 --> 00:20:48,260
“You can raise it that high now? How did it improve so suddenly?”

136
00:20:48,260 --> 00:21:00,680
I hadn't seen that therapist in about two weeks. “Mukai, your diligent daily exercises are paying off,” they said.

137
00:21:00,680 --> 00:21:05,600
They sounded moved as they congratulated me.

138
00:21:05,600 --> 00:21:10,280
I haven't done anything, though. I thought, “Oh no.” Why can I raise it now?

139
00:21:10,280 --> 00:21:17,720
I haven't done anything. Have you stopped doing anything? Changed what you carry, your bag, your posture? No.

140
00:21:17,720 --> 00:21:30,580
Natural healing. It might even have healed on its own. Maybe. But the therapist was really moved.

141
00:21:30,580 --> 00:21:40,320
With frozen shoulder, whether it happens in your forties or fifties, the cause isn't very clear. We don't know exactly why it happens, and when it improves varies from person to person.

142
00:21:40,320 --> 00:21:51,420
Maybe you'll be able to go back to jiu-jitsu soon. Maybe. You're still in good health, aren't you, Hondo?

143
00:21:51,420 --> 00:22:04,000
I've had shoulder stiffness ever since I got back from America. The thing you mentioned before? That prickly, itchy feeling from inside.

144
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:13,820
I'm steadily trying to fix my rounded shoulders. Mine are really rolled forward. That's when your shoulders sit forward. My shoulders should actually look wider.

145
00:22:13,820 --> 00:22:22,960
I hold a bar or dumbbells. With a bar, I imagine lowering it behind my head.

146
00:22:22,960 --> 00:22:31,140
I lower my arms and squeeze my shoulder blades together whenever I remember. A little at a time.

147
00:22:31,140 --> 00:22:40,720
I really think health is incredibly important. It's scary now.

148
00:22:40,720 --> 00:22:51,420
If I wake up with a slight headache, all the kanji that come to mind are terrifying. I know what you mean.

149
00:22:51,420 --> 00:23:01,100
“Could it be that?” Those illnesses written entirely in kanji, with no hiragana. I keep thinking, “What if it's that?” That's how it is at 46.

150
00:23:01,100 --> 00:23:08,740
Forty-six, huh? I never imagined being 46 would be like this. Did you think you'd be healthier?

151
00:23:09,420 --> 00:23:22,340
Health is part of it, but I thought I'd be surrounded by more things and people I love.

152
00:23:22,340 --> 00:23:34,460
Like a wife, a daughter, a son, a dog. Maybe even keeping medaka fish. Tropical fish... [unclear].

153
00:23:34,460 --> 00:23:41,180
A small but newly built house. New construction! Gardening in the yard.

154
00:23:41,180 --> 00:23:50,500
Maybe people would say it doesn't suit me, but a left-hand-drive car. You've got it all together.

155
00:23:50,500 --> 00:24:00,000
Getting along with the neighbors, passing around neighborhood notices, PTA meetings. Busy! That's what I imagined.

156
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:14,400
In reality, it's anime, anime, anime, anime. Nothing's changed since I was 14. Exactly the same for 32 years.

157
00:24:14,400 --> 00:24:23,740
But your appearance has changed. You've started parting your bangs, and your clothes are getting more refined. That's good.

158
00:24:23,740 --> 00:24:38,780
When I tell younger comedians in their fourth or fifth year how old I am, they're really surprised. You look young. Enough to make them go, “Forty-six?!”

159
00:24:38,780 --> 00:24:46,800
Maybe I'll go with an “ageless beauty” persona now. Abracadabra! You're focusing on the “witch” part of the word.

160
00:24:46,800 --> 00:25:00,900
I'll get a face beautiful enough to live up to that description. I'm getting paler and paler. Plastic surgery might be an option.

161
00:25:00,900 --> 00:25:12,460
We only managed to read two messages. Well, let's read more another time. We can next week too. We'll read whatever we want! That's a good thing.

162
00:25:12,460 --> 00:25:21,840
Listeners, please keep joining the operation by sending messages. Time to wrap up. We look forward to hearing from you.

163
00:25:21,840 --> 00:25:32,700
For postcards, write to Bunka Hoso, Hondo Landing Operation, postal code 105-8002. For email, use all lowercase: sakusen@joqr.net.

164
00:25:32,700 --> 00:25:39,580
You can also use the message form on the show's website. Send us a message and join the operation.

165
00:25:39,580 --> 00:25:48,440
The show's official X account is “HJ Sakusen.” Our comments and photos are posted after the broadcast, so please follow us.

166
00:25:48,440 --> 00:25:55,480
Let's do the announcements. Screenshot, screenshot! I'm taking a screenshot.

167
00:25:55,480 --> 00:26:01,380
Meitantei Precure! and The Classroom of a Black Cat and a Witch. Sorry if any of these have already had their finales.

168
00:26:01,380 --> 00:26:03,940
[Unclear title prefix] Isekai Gohantabi.

169
00:26:03,940 --> 00:26:09,200
The World's Strongest Rearguard, Degarashi Ouji, Iruma-kun, Daemons of the Shadow Realm, and That Time I Got Reincarnated as a Slime.

170
00:26:09,200 --> 00:26:16,700
It's had its finale, but there's BanG Dream! Yume Mita. Also Saijo no Osewa and Spider-Man: Brand New Day.

171
00:26:16,700 --> 00:26:24,080
I also appear partway through Ascendance of a Bookworm. Please check them out. You really are a busy voice actress.

172
00:26:24,080 --> 00:26:37,880
There's a Yume Mita special. Has it aired already? Not yet? It's next week, so it may be over by the time this airs. It might be on TVer.

173
00:26:37,880 --> 00:26:44,620
I'm hosting the special, and Hondo appears as a guest too. Please check it out.

174
00:26:45,240 --> 00:26:53,980
I'm doing a dramatic-reading event on September 21 at Yoshimoto Makuhari Aeon Mall Theater. Please check X and elsewhere for details.

175
00:26:53,980 --> 00:27:03,200
The show's archives are available on YouTube. New merchandise is on sale at the A&G shop. Please take a look.

176
00:27:03,200 --> 00:27:13,120
With the director and writer away, it's nice having these relaxed conversations that explore one topic. It is. We haven't had that feeling in a while.

177
00:27:13,120 --> 00:27:20,920
We both talked quite a bit, didn't we? I talked plenty too, right? I wonder how the balance came across.

178
00:27:20,920 --> 00:27:28,120
We'll have to hear what you think in the YouTube comments. Please be kind. We'd appreciate it.

179
00:27:28,120 --> 00:27:36,380
Personally, I think it's fine to talk at length. I agree. Sorry, really.

180
00:27:36,380 --> 00:27:50,920
About how I apply hair gel, someone said, “Just put it on the ends.” Thank you. I'm sure you're saying it out of concern for me. Thanks.

181
00:27:50,920 --> 00:28:01,800
It's my first time getting hairstyle advice in the comments. Apparently I shouldn't put it on the roots. So I've heard.

182
00:28:01,800 --> 00:28:11,100
I saw that in the YouTube comments. “It makes you look shiny.” I don't mind looking shiny, though. Anyway, thank you.

183
00:28:11,100 --> 00:28:18,440
We welcome your thoughts in the comments, with the “本渡上陸作戦” hashtag, and by email. Please let us know.

184
00:28:18,440 --> 00:28:27,380
Your hosts were Kaede Hondo, enjoying being blonde, and Hantaro Tenshin, enjoying putting gel on his roots.

185
00:28:27,380 --> 00:28:30,480
Hondo Landing Operation. This mission is now complete.

